===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|facing side}}The foremost [[side]] of something or the [[end]] that [[face]]s the direction it normally [[move]]s.
# The side of a [[building]] with the main [[entrance]].
#: {{ux|en|I'll go out the '''front''', and you go out the back.}}
#: {{ux|en|Flowering plants cascaded down the '''fronts''' of the buildings.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=Pretty soon I struck into a sort of path{{nb...}}. It twisted and turned,{{...}}and opened out into a big clear space like a lawn. And, back of the lawn, was a big, old-fashioned house, with piazzas stretching in '''front''' of it, and all blazing with lights.}}
# A [[field]] of [[activity]].
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|date=2012-01|author=Stephen Ledoux|volume=100|issue=1|page=60|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=Behaviorism at 100|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2012/1/behaviorism-at-100|passage=Becoming more aware of the progress that scientists have made on behavioral '''fronts''' can reduce the risk that other natural scientists will resort to mystical agential accounts when they exceed the limits of their own disciplinary training.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20131110010832/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2012/1/behaviorism-at-100|archivedate=10 November 2013}}
# {{senseid|en|person or group acting as public face of covert group}} A person or institution acting as the [[public]] face of some other, [[covert]] group.
#: {{nearsyn|en|frontperson<id:person acting as public face of covert group>|frontman<id:person acting as public face of covert group>|front man<id:person acting as public face of covert group>}}
#: {{ux|en|Officially it's a dry-cleaning shop, but everyone knows it's a '''front''' for the mafia.}}
# {{lb|en|meteorology}} The [[interface]] or [[transition zone]] between two [[airmass]]es of different [[density]], often resulting in [[precipitation]]. Since the [[temperature]] [[distribution]] is the most important [[regulator]] of [[atmospheric]] density, a front almost invariably separates airmasses of different temperature.
#: {{ux|en|We need to take the clothes off the [[line]]. The news reported a '''front''' is coming in from the east, and we can expect heavy rain and maybe [[hail]].}}
# {{lb|en|military}} An [[area]] where [[army|armies]] are engaged in [[conflict]], especially the [[line of contact]].
# {{lb|en|military}} The [[lateral]] space occupied by an [[element]] measured from the extremity of one [[flank]] to the extremity of the other flank.
# {{lb|en|military}} The direction of the [[enemy]].
# {{lb|en|military}} When a [[combat]] situation does not exist or is not assumed, the direction toward which the [[command]] is faced.
# {{lb|en|historical}} A major military subdivision of the [[Soviet]] Army.
# {{lb|en|dated}} [[cheek|Cheek]]; [[boldness]]; [[impudence]].
# {{lb|en|dated|euphemistic|chiefly|in the plural}} A woman's [[breast]].
#* {{quote-book|en|text={{...}} there was one bare breast sticking out, the tip of it disappearing into Enid's father's mouth. She had told her mother about this in perfect certainty that she had seen it. She said, "One of her '''fronts''' was stuck in Daddy's mouth."|author=Alice Munro|title=The Love of a Good Woman|page=74|year=1998}}
#* {{quote-book|en|text=She thought—imagined, really—that her, well, that her '''fronts''' were too big. She grew up, you see, in the flapper era, when it was fashionable for women to have very flat '''fronts'''.|title=The Wrong Kind of Money|year=2015|author=Stephen Birmingham}}
#* {{quote-web|en|title=Wings And A Pair|site={{w|The Onion}}|url=https://theonion.com/wings-and-a-pair-1839045632/|author={{w|Ward Sutton}} as Stan Kelly|text=Turning their Backs... ON '''FRONTS'''.<br/><small>CONVIVIAL WAITRESS:</small> D... Don't you want to ogle me?<br/><small>SICKO MILLENNIAL:</small> No... I hate breasts!|date=2019-10-15}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} An [[act]], [[show]], [[façade]], [[persona]]: an intentional and false impression of oneself.
#: {{ux|en|He says he likes hip-hop, but I think it's just a '''front'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|You don't need to put on a '''front'''. Just be yourself.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|I|vi|passage=with smiling '''fronts''' encountering}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|chapter=13|passage=The inhabitants showed a bold '''front'''.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} That which covers the foremost part of the head: a front piece of false hair worn by women.
#* {{RQ:Browning Aurora Leigh|text=like any plain Miss Smith's, who wears a '''front'''}}
# The most [[conspicuous]] part.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|I|i|passage=the very head and '''front''' of my offending}}
# The [[beginning]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|102|text=summer's '''front'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Kenneth Womack; Todd F. Davis|title=Reading the Beatles|page=43
|passage=So the faulty bridge was moved to the '''front''' of the song, creating in the process one of the most striking opening moments in Beatles music.}}
# {{lb|en|UK}} A [[seafront]] or coastal [[promenade]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The forehead or brow, the part of the face above the eyes; sometimes, also, the whole face.
#* {{RQ:Pope Dunciad|passage=Bless'd with his father's '''front''', his mother's tongue.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|I|i|passage=Grim-visaged war hath smoothed his wrinkled '''front'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1700|author=w:Matthew Prior|title=Seeing the Duke of Ormond's Picture at Sir Godfrey Kneller's
|passage=His '''front''' yet threatens, and his frowns command.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|hotels|dated}} The [[bellhop]] whose turn it is to answer a [[client]]'s [[call]], which is often the word "front" used as an exclamation.
# {{lb|en|slang|in the plural}} A [[grill]] {{gloss|jewellery worn on front teeth}}.
#* {{quote-av|en|director=w:Barry Jenkins|actor=w:André Holland|role=Kevin|title=[[w:Moonlight (2016 film)|Moonlight]]|at=1:41:25|year=2016|passage=I'm saying, man, them '''fronts'''? That car? Who ''is'' you, Chiron?}}

